  "interpretation": "<table style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 85.8344%; height: 216.881px;\" border=\"1\"><colgroup><col style=\"width: 19.2035%;\"><col style=\"width: 9.60536%;\"><col style=\"width: 10.7423%;\"><col style=\"width: 11.0678%;\"><col style=\"width: 11.2306%;\"><col style=\"width: 38.0863%;\"></colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 32.983px;\">\n<td rowspan=\"2\">Undiluted ( 1:1 )</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" colspan=\"4\">&nbsp;</td>\n<td rowspan=\"2\">Report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 18.983px;\">\n<td>1:2</td>\n<td>1:4</td>\n<td>1:8</td>\n<td>1:16</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32.983px;\">\n<td>Rm</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Reactive, undiluted1:1, or R1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32.983px;\">\n<td>R</td>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Recative,1:2 dilution, or R2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32.983px;\">\n<td>R</td>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Reactive, 1:4 dilution, or R4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32.983px;\">\n<td>R</td>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>R</td>\n<td>Rm</td>\n<td>N</td>\n<td>Reactive,1:8 dilution, or R8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 32.983px;\">\n<td style=\"text-align: center;\" colspan=\"6\">R = Reactive , Rm = minimally reactive, N=Non reactive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>REMARK:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>This test is done for qualitative and semi-quantitative analysis of syphilis.</li>\n<li>The results suggest highest dilution that has given a reactive result including a minimally reactive result.</li>\n<li>False negative results can be seen in early and latent stages of disease.</li>\n<li>False positive may occur during a variety of acute and chronic conditions such as malaria, leprosy, infectious mononucleosis and viral pneumonia and other upper respiratory diseases as well as collagen and immunological diseases like rheumatoid arthritis, lupus erythmatosis etc.</li>\n<li>Reactive results must be correlated with supporting clinical features, history and epidemiological evidence to arrive at a final diagnosis.</li>\n</ul>",
